course: dictionary
lesson: 6215
title: Mistake: one word, two jobs
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A mistake is something wrong, like a wrong answer in maths or a spelling that is not right. Everyone makes them, even teachers. «Everybody makes mistakes, even teachers» tells you that making a mistake is normal, not something to be ashamed of.
explain: Mistake can also be a verb, which means to think one person or thing is another. «I mistook him for his brother» means I thought he was his brother, but I was wrong. Mistook is the past tense of mistake, so watch for it in stories too.
explain: Look at the words around mistake to find its meaning. «By mistake» means without meaning to. The clue is by mistake: she did not plan it, so it was an accident.
explain: Mistakes often help us learn. Check your work for mistakes, correct them, and you will get better. A girl in Perth who knocks over a glass jar and tells the teacher straight away is showing courage, because owning up is hard.
fact: Mistake as a noun means something wrong, such as a wrong answer or a wrong action.
fact: Mistake as a verb means to think one person or thing is another.
fact: «By mistake» means without meaning to.
fact: «Mistakes were made» admits that something went wrong but does not say who did it.
fact: Mistook, mistaken and mistaking are other forms of the verb mistake.
exercise: «Everybody makes mistakes, even teachers.» What does mistake mean in this sentence? | Something you never do | A holiday trip | A kind of game | * Something wrong, like a wrong answer
exercise: «I mistook him for his brother.» What does mistook mean here? | Chose a gift for him | * Thought one person was another | Hugged him warmly | Forgot his name
exercise: «She broke the vase by mistake.» What does by mistake mean? | * Without meaning to | In a hurry to win | On purpose, very carefully
test: «Mistakes were made» is a phrase that does what? | Means a story has a happy ending | * Admits something went wrong but does not say who did it | Says exactly who was to blame
test: Which word is a past tense of the verb mistake? | Mistrust | Mistakes | * Mistook
exam: «Correcting someone in front of everyone often hurts more than the mistake itself.» What does this sentence suggest? | * Telling someone about their mistake in public can upset them more than the mistake did | Mistakes never hurt anyone | Only teachers make mistakes | Mistakes should always be shouted about
